# Does a review policy fit the taxonomy it was written in?
#
# Protocol: the manuscript before the style revision was given to two independent reviewers, together
# with the same style checklist and nothing else; neither saw the author's decisions, the repository,
# or the other reviewer. They agreed on 14 of 16 findings (Cohen's kappa 0.67). The author's own
# decisions, recorded when the revision was made, agree with them on 11 and 9 of the 16 (kappa below
# zero): the author's labels are the outlier, so they are not treated as a policy here.
#
# This scenario uses the 14 findings the two reviewers agreed on. Five of them were adopted and nine
# rejected; the identifiers below are the order of the consensus list, not the original numbering:
#   0  reject  abstract: states a gap?            (both reviewers: the 'yet' clause already does)
#   1  adopt   percentage without a base
#   2  adopt   citations use an ordinary space
#   3  adopt   section references mix two forms
#   4  adopt   "within a few percent" against 2.10/1.85
#   5  reject  section title "Experiments"
#   6  adopt   concept-lattice caption silent about the lattice
#   7  adopt   "exhausted" vs "insufficient" for the same state
#   8  adopt   hyphens used as parenthetical dashes
#   9  adopt   acronyms never expanded
#  10  adopt   r used for two quantities
#  11  adopt   measured runs retold in the present tense
#  12  adopt   voice splits inside the experiments section
#  13  reject  "proved incomplete" reports a probe as a proof
#
# The predicates are the taxonomy the findings came from: which part of the paper each one touches.

objects 14
add f_abstract 0
add f_numbers 1 4
add f_xref 2 3
add f_titles 5
add f_captions 6
add f_terminology 7
add f_typography 8 9
add f_notation 10
add f_voice 11 12 13

vote 0 0 3
vote 1 3 0
vote 2 3 0
vote 3 3 0
vote 4 3 0
vote 5 0 3
vote 6 3 0
vote 7 3 0
vote 8 3 0
vote 9 3 0
vote 10 3 0
vote 11 3 0
vote 12 3 0
vote 13 0 3

need            # expect INSUFFICIENT / PROPOSE, colliding pairs (11,13) and (12,13)

# The taxonomy has one hole: it cannot tell a claim-about-wording from a claim-about-strength.
# The two reviewers rejected "proved incomplete" because a probe refutes deductively, and adopted
# the tense and voice findings because they are register; both sit in f_voice.
add claim_strength 13

need            # expect IDENTIFIED / STOP, colliding pairs none
